@mainpage Overview

[TOC]

> This is the homepage of **TRIQS/@PROJECT_NAME@ v@PROJECT_VERSION@**. The source code can be found on [GitHub](https://github.com/TRIQS/inchworm).

**inchworm** is a TRIQS-based quantum impurity solver. It implements an equilibrium,
imaginary-time Inchworm quantum Monte Carlo solver for quantum impurity models, based on a
continuous-time hybridization expansion and built on the
[TRIQS](https://github.com/TRIQS/triqs) library.

It iteratively computes the imaginary-time impurity propagator @f$\mathcal{U}(\tau)@f$ and
samples the impurity Green's function @f$G(\tau)@f$ for general local interactions, expressed
as TRIQS many-body operators, and block-structured, matrix-valued hybridization functions.
Monte Carlo sampling is MPI-parallel, and both C++ and Python interfaces are provided.

This documentation is generated based on [doxygen](https://www.doxygen.nl/)
and the comments in the sources and headers.

@section start Where to start?

The @ref installation section tells you how to get inchworm and make it available on your system.

@ref integration explains how to integrate **inchworm** into your own C++ code.

Then, the @ref examples section shows how **inchworm** can be used in practice.

Furthermore, we provide a detailed reference @ref documentation.

If you experience any problem with the library, you can post an [issue](../issues.html) on GitHub.
